WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.400
♪His name is Rio
and he watches from the stands♪

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.200
was how football fans
greeted Rio Ferdinand

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.480
when he was banned
for missing a drugs test.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.240
Some football chants
are made up on the spot,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.160
some have echoed around
the same grounds for generations.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.200
But scientists think
they are a practice

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.440
that has deep roots in human culture.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.680
Every social group ever studied,
from the Maori Hakka

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.520
to the Sufi Whirling Dervishes

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.920
to awkward British
people at a wedding,

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.160
engages in some form of group
singing, dancing, and chanting.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:33.640
Why is this?

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.080
Psychologists have found

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.040
that when a group of people
engage in collective behaviour -

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.400
like chanting -

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.400
it has a profound effect
on how they think and feel.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.160
Group singing's been shown
to have a powerful effect

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:46.920
on the mood of depressed people.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.640
As well as the uplifting music,

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.720
it seems that synchronising
your breathing,

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.760
heartrate and voice with other people

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:56.760
has the remarkable effect of making
us feel connected to one another.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.560
But of course football fans
don't usually feel connected

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.720
to everyone in the stadium.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.720
There are plenty of examples
of football chants that are rude,

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.480
offensive, even racist or homophobic.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.160
Though they've historically been
a means to abuse players

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:12.200
and referees,

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.680
they can also endorse
more positive values

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.440
like celebrating striker
Mo Salah's goals with,

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.520
♪If he scores another few
then I'll be Muslim, too♪

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.800
Collective behaviour like chanting
is, more than anything,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.640
a way to express social identity
and the values of that group.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.720
Like the Liverpool fans chanting,
"Justice for the 96"

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.760
in support of the Hillsborough
disaster victims.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.080
Chanting at a football match doesn’t
just bond a group together

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.080
in love and harmony,

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:45.160
there is a dark side
to coordinated behaviour.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.720
Most armies around the world
march up and down in parades.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.080
For the past 100 years,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.800
at least since the invention
of the machine gun,

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:58.080
it's been a bad idea to walk slowly
towards the enemy in a straight line.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.560
So why do soldiers
march and chant together?

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.280
In one experiment,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.720
people were asked to put a jar
of live woodlice into a grinder.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.440
The people who had marched in unison
around the car park beforehand

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.600
threw about 50% more
of the bugs into the grinder

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.520
than those who had
walked the same distance

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.600
but not in a coordinated march.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.520
Please note there was actually
an escape chute in the grinder

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.640
and every bug escaped unharmed.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.400
This suggests coordinated behaviours

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.040
can lower people's sense
of personal autonomy,

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.400
and make them more likely
to be obedient to aggressive action.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.440
And there is a final reason
that people might chant

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.680
at a football match.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.960
Writing has only been around
for a few thousand years,

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.920
but we find chants and songs
in every pre-literate culture.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.600
Even today in India,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.760
it's the chanted version
of Vedic texts

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.000
that's seen as the definitive
version, not the written form.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.080
Perhaps chanting is a call back
to those older practises of encoding

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.080
and sharing cultural knowledge.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.520
So next time you hear
a thousand people yelling...

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:01.920
to the referee,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.360
remember that it's because
they're bonding with each other,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.240
affirming their social identity

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.160
and using an ancient technology
to record knowledge.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:14.120
And, perhaps, because the referee
doesn’t know what he's doing.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.700
Thanks for watching! :)

